  "narrative": "During the 1920s, Black and African women's hairstyles and hair culture were deeply rooted in tradition and identity. While specific details about this decade are scarce, we can infer that hairstyles such as cornrows, a style of three-strand braids close to the scalp, were likely prevalent. Cornrows were considered a traditional hairstyle in many African cultures and the African diaspora, showcasing the rich cultural heritage of the time. The Jheri curl, however, was not introduced until the 1980s, so it is unlikely that it was a popular style during the 1920s. Instead, women may have opted for more natural styles like Bantu knots or wore their hair in its natural texture, embracing the unique beauty of kinky hair. The concept of protective hairstyles as we know it today also predates this era, but it's possible that women from this time period recognized the importance of minimizing manipulation and exposure to environmental elements to preserve their hair's health.",
